From: winvm runtime <noreply@stuffbucket.co>
Subject: [PATCH] vhost-user: inject the kick through the host notifier

Since "vhost-user: inject kick after SET_VRING_KICK" (ef02c6becf, QEMU
11.1), vhost_user_set_vring_kick() writes a kick into file->fd. That fd
is event_notifier_get_fd() of the virtqueue's host notifier: the read
side. Where eventfd exists both sides are one descriptor, so this works.
Without eventfd (macOS, the BSDs) an EventNotifier is a pipe, the write
fails with EBADF, vhost_dev_start() fails ("vhost_set_vring_kick failed:
Bad file descriptor (9)"), and no vhost-user device can start.

Find the host notifier whose read side file->fd is and kick it with
event_notifier_set(), which writes to the write side. Fall back to the
old write for any other fd.

Carried by winvm-runtime for vhost-user-fs-pci (virtiofs) until upstream
QEMU has an equivalent fix.
---
--- a/hw/virtio/vhost-user.c
+++ b/hw/virtio/vhost-user.c
@@ -1485,6 +1485,24 @@
     }
 
     return 0;
+}
+
+/*
+ * The host notifier whose read side is file->fd, or NULL. vhost-user's
+ * vhost_get_vq_index() is the identity, so file->index is the device's
+ * virtqueue index.
+ */
+static EventNotifier *vhost_user_kick_notifier(struct vhost_dev *dev,
+                                               struct vhost_vring_file *file)
+{
+    EventNotifier *notifier;
+
+    if (!dev->vdev || file->index >= VIRTIO_QUEUE_MAX) {
+        return NULL;
+    }
+    notifier = virtio_queue_get_host_notifier(
+        virtio_get_queue(dev->vdev, file->index));
+    return event_notifier_get_fd(notifier) == file->fd ? notifier : NULL;
 }
 
 static int vhost_user_set_vring_kick(struct vhost_dev *dev,
@@ -1500,8 +1518,20 @@
      * receiving a kick. The spec suggests this to improve compatibility.
      */
     if (file->fd != -1) {
+        EventNotifier *notifier = vhost_user_kick_notifier(dev, file);
         uint64_t val = 1;
         ssize_t nwritten;
+
+        /*
+         * Kick through the notifier when file->fd is its read side. Without
+         * eventfd (macOS, the BSDs) an EventNotifier is a pipe: file->fd is
+         * the read end, which is what the back-end polls, and writing to it
+         * fails with EBADF. event_notifier_set() writes to the write end,
+         * which is the same descriptor where eventfd exists.
+         */
+        if (notifier) {
+            return event_notifier_set(notifier);
+        }
 
         do {
             nwritten = write(file->fd, &val, sizeof(val));
